    "result_text": "A claim now belongs to the period that contains its due-by, not its date. `bin/e2e` passes all 4 tests.\n\n**Server**\n- **Totals:** period totals and each person's period figures count a claim by its due-by. This also changes the totals fixed when a period is closed or soft-closed.\n- **Numbering:** per-period numbers use the period of the due-by. In the test, a claim dated 2026-02-25 that is due 2026-03-11 gets `P2-3`.\n- **Closed periods:** a claim is refused for opening, settling, voiding, editing, splitting and similar actions only when its due-by is in a closed period. This applies to the single actions and the bulk ones.\n- **New claims:** adding a claim, quick-add and paste all work out the due-by the claim would get (its date plus its terms). A claim is refused if that date falls in a closed period, and marked a late entry if it falls in a soft-closed one. For quick-add, I moved this check to after the person is looked up, because the terms depend on the person. It still runs before anything is written to the audit file.\n- **Split:** the split-off claim is now marked a late entry if its due-by is in a soft-closed period. The test expects this. No `LATE_ENTRY` audit line is written for it, because the test doesn't expect one.\n\n**Two removals.** Since the due-by never changes after a claim is added, a claim's period can't change either. So:\n- **Re-date** no longer refuses a new date inside a closed period, and no longer gives the claim a new number. It keeps its period and number.\n- **Editing a draft** no longer checks whether the new date is in a closed period. Only the existing claim's due-by is checked.\n\n`RefuseClosedDate` is no longer used in any pipeline; I kept it only as the shared check and error message.\n\n**Front end:** the claim's period, the period and per-person totals, and the editor's instant \"closed period\" refusal all use `dueBy` instead of `date`.\n\nNo database migration was needed, because `due_by` is already stored on every claim and can't be null.",
